// The Ancient Alphabets of Ibn Waḥshiyya — five of the book's tables drawn
// from Hammer's pages of 1806, and two of its tables of hieroglyphs shown as
// printed.
//
// From the wing's research file ibn-wahshiyya.verified.json, whose researcher
// and fact-checker could not search the web, read against Hammer's volume:
// first the Getty Research Institute copy at the Internet Archive and the text
// of Hämeen-Anttila 2006, then, page by page, the Wellcome Library copy
// (Internet Archive b22039910, Public Domain Mark). That reading — the
// catalogue of the volume's 86 tables, the manifest of the pages used and the
// findings that settle the page's earlier open points — is kept in
// docs/research/magical-scriptorium/plates/ibn-wahshiyya.catalogue.json,
// .manifest.json and .source-and-trace.json.
//
// The five tables (the Syriac pen, Arabic p. 9; Aflāṭūn's, p. 18; Hermes Abū
// Ṭāṭ's, p. 34; Siyūriyānūs's, p. 36; Jupiter's, p. 48) were traced from the
// Wellcome copy's page images at full size and checked sign by sign, by
// independent checkers, against the Getty and North Carolina copies
// (docs/research/magical-scriptorium/traced/ibn-wahshiyya/<id>.glyphs.json).
// Every glyph here is the checked path data unchanged; the notes keep what
// tells the reader something (a copy difference, an ambiguity, a detail the
// pen cannot keep). The two pictorial tables of hieroglyphs (the Shīshīm pen
// of the Harāmisa, Arabic pp. 119–121, and the first series, English p. 19)
// are shown as plates, not drawn. All the plates are the Wellcome copy's.
//
// What the pages settled is stated with its Arabic page: the count of tables,
// the sages who are not Greek, Hermes among the seven sages, the table of
// letter-valued hieroglyphs (in the closing section, thirty-eight letters by
// its own count), the tables not fully keyed, the tree alphabets, the
// planets', the zodiac's and the kings' alphabets, the date of deposit and
// the colophon, and Hammer's misprinted references. Still left out: an
// "alphabet of the Kabbalists" (no such heading in the Arabic or in Hammer's
// English contents), the date "1835" for Hammer's later name, the seven-seals
// poem (its Arabic is to be taken from Winkler or Canaan, as the charaktêres
// page also says) and the Kircher question. What the pages leave open — the
// thirty-seven signs counted against the thirty-eight the book states, the
// names under the last row of p. 121 — is given as open. Renderings of the
// Arabic are this page's own, and are marked so; Hammer's English is quoted
// as printed.
