    family: 'Not a single alphabet: a book, ascribed to Ibn Waḥshiyya, that catalogues many scripts — the scripts of earlier peoples, alphabets it credits to Hermes and to named sages, alphabets of the planets, of the twelve signs of the zodiac and of ancient kings, and the Egyptian hieroglyphs, which it reads as signs for things and ideas, beside a table of hieroglyph-like signs it gives the values of letters. It belongs to the Arabic literature of the aqlām, the “pens” or scripts. Scholars connect its ring-ended alphabets of the sages, like the seven seals of Arabic talismanic practice, with the charaktêres of late-antique magic.',
