                about: 'The first of the three series (marātib) that follow the celestial signs, and so the second of the book’s four tables of hieroglyphs read as signs of things and ideas (Hammer’s English, p. 16: “It is divided into three series, beside the celestial or supernatural objects.”). All four stand under Hermes the great, whose pen, the book says, is not, like the other pens, set out on the letters, but is symbols and signs (Arabic p. 81, this page’s rendering; Hammer’s English, p. 16: “It does not consist in a series of letters like other alphabets, but in expressions composed according to the arrangement made by Hermes the great.”). Hammer reprints the series over four pages, English pp. 19–22, with an English word under each sign; the page shown is the first of the four. The Arabic part prints the same series, set in other rows, with its Arabic words, on pp. 85–89, under the heading المرتبة الاولي في ذكر الاسما الحيوانية واشكالها مفرداً مفرداً . من ساير الانواع وافعالها وحركاتها (p. 85). El Daly links the signs read as things and ideas to the determinatives of Egyptian writing, which mark the sense of a word; whether that is insight, or the old Greek notion of hieroglyphs as symbols — the notion of Horapollo’s Hieroglyphica — in an Arabic setting, is the heart of the dispute.',
